.TH "jeopardy\-validate" "1" "October 5 2026" "jeopardy 0.1.0" "CIC Commands Manual"
.SH NAME
.PP
jeopardy\-validate \(en check Jeopardy question sets
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
jeopardy\-validate \f[I]file\f[R]\&...
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[B]jeopardy\-validate\f[R] loads each question set and reports whether
the server would accept it.
A set must match the format in \f[B]jeopardy\-questions\f[R](5) and pass
these checks:
.IP \(bu 2
there is at least one category;
.IP \(bu 2
every category has the same number of clues, and at least one;
.IP \(bu 2
clue values are positive and ascending within a category;
.IP \(bu 2
no clue is already marked as answered.
.PP
A set without a final clue is accepted, with a warning.
.PP
For each file, one line is printed: \f[B]ok\f[R] with the title and
counts, or \f[B]FAIL\f[R] with the reason.
Warnings follow an \f[B]ok\f[R] line.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
\f[B]0\f[R]
All files are valid.
.TP
\f[B]1\f[R]
At least one file failed.
.PP
Running it with no arguments prints a usage message and exits with
status 1.
.SH EXAMPLES
.IP
.EX
$ jeopardy\-validate games/sample.yaml
ok   games/sample.yaml: \(aqSample Game\(aq, 6 categories, 30 clues
.EE
